WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:04.060
We have this sense that survival
is like, "Hold on to your hat,"

00:00:04.060 --> 00:00:04.560
right?

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.730
"Here it comes."

00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:08.290
I don't think of it that way.

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.630
I'm a former Air
Force SERE instructor,

00:00:10.630 --> 00:00:13.680
which is survival, evasion,
resistance, and escape.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.570
We are the people who
teach our soldiers what

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:17.580
to do if they come
down behind enemy lines

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.030
and they want to
get back home again.

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.740
Over my 30 years or so,
of teaching these skills,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.170
this is really the most
consolidated version I've done,

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:27.545
and many of the skills you're
going to see me doing here,

00:00:27.545 --> 00:00:31.400
I've never done
anywhere else on camera.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:33.800
The core of the class is going
to revolve around the five

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.450
basic needs of a survivor.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:39.380
Signaling, personal protection,
sustenance, navigation,

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.740
and health.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:43.340
We'll talk about what to do
before you leave on a trip, how

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.257
you should be thinking
about things before you

00:00:45.257 --> 00:00:46.790
enter into the wilderness.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.830
Then we're going to
make a survival kit.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.720
Day hikes are the
most dangerous,

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.000
because we don't tend to
take that much with us.

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.920
What I put in a survival
kit can literally

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:56.710
make the difference
between life and death

00:00:56.710 --> 00:00:59.300
if I find myself stuck out
there for a longer period.

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.280
In a survival
situation we need to be

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:03.480
able to think rationally
and not panic.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.590
A key way to do this is
simply make your exhales

00:01:06.590 --> 00:01:09.470
longer than your inhales, and
when things start to go wrong

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.110
my number one priority
is to get out of here,

00:01:12.110 --> 00:01:14.540
so signaling needs
to be A-number one.

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:17.060
If your mind was going
through, oh, I need water,

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:20.300
I'm going to need a fire,
those are all secondary.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:23.750
We can go, typically,
about 30 days without food,

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.570
and on average, about
three days without water.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:31.080
When I was a kid growing
up I had some abuse issues.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:34.640
And as I got older, I would
start to sneak out of the house

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.160
when I would have
nightmares and flashbacks.

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:39.800
I didn't want to be in a house
that didn't feel safe to me.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.100
I wanted to go outside,
and I'd go climb a tree.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.290
That was my safe zone.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.930
The wilderness is so amazing
to help us be capable,

00:01:47.930 --> 00:01:50.120
competent human beings.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:51.620
What I really want
you to understand

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.570
is that the wilderness
is not out to get you.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:56.480
She's not this creature that's
hovering and just waiting

00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:58.220
for an opportunity to pounce.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.230
So relax that mindset,
and go out there

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:02.240
and play, and learn
these techniques.

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:05.580
It'll open up a whole
new world for you.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:10.300
I'm Jessie Krebs, and
this is my MasterClass.

